  "entry": {
    "id": "one-person-sixty-cars",
    "title": "One person. An entire car.",
    "topic": "transportation",
    "format": "deep-dive",
    "status": "review",
    "order": 80,
    "question": "How much vehicle does it take to move one person?",
    "answer": "Sharing a vehicle spreads its space and mass across more people. Sixty solo drivers need sixty cars; four per car need fifteen.",
    "explanation": "An interactive essay separating vehicle occupancy, street capacity, fuel-energy conversion and illustrative vehicle mass per traveler.",
    "qualification": "Vehicle counts are an illustrative model, not measured road area or real-world occupancy. Capacity ranges describe peak conditions; energy estimates describe specified test cycles. Mass per person is not energy efficiency or a lifecycle footprint.",
    "whyCare": "A street can be full of vehicles while doing surprisingly little to move people.",
    "evidence": {
      "kind": "calculation",
      "scope": "Illustrative group of 60 travelers with 1, 2 or 4 people per car, including drivers; one bus with 60 passengers, excluding its driver. Global Street Design Guide capacity ranges for a 3-m lane or equivalent width under peak conditions with normal operations. U.S. DOE/EPA test-cycle energy estimates. Illustrative 2,000 kg car, excluding occupants. Separate traffic state: 32 solo drivers plus 60 bus passengers (92 travelers), with one additional bus operator. Original illustrative dense neighborhood model; qualitative land-use and transit relationships from EPA guidance.",
      "dataAsOf": "2016 street design guide, reproduced in 2018 presentation; DOE EV summary September 16, 2024; undated DOE/EPA gasoline page; original illustrative model October 2026; EPA smart-growth guidance updated April 30, 2026",
      "checkedAt": "2026-10-06",
      "reviewDue": "2027-04-06",
      "denominator": "People per vehicle; people per hour in a 3-m lane or equivalent width; percent of fuel energy in the gasoline vehicle; kg of illustrative vehicle per occupant. These denominators are not interchangeable.",
      "methodology": "Vehicle counts: 60/n for n=1,2,4; bus illustration assumes 60 passengers. Mass allocation: 2000/n kg for n=1,2,3,4 (rounded only for display). Capacity ranges copied as factual values from Prati 2018 slide 23 (PDF page 23), with 3-m scope supplied by the Global Street Design Guide. Gasoline 18–25% energy to wheels in combined 55% city/45% highway test cycle. EV 87–91% includes net regenerative recovery; excludes lifecycle and electricity generation. No claim that 96% of energy is wasted moving vehicle mass. No causal congestion or citywide emissions estimate. Traffic scene: 32 solo cars + one bus carrying 60 passengers = 33 vehicles and 92 travelers; bus operator additional. This is a separate population from the fixed-60 comparison. Neighborhood camera views are conceptual design illustrations with no modeled travel time, capacity, density, energy or emissions output.",
      "sources": [
