lass=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"479.10\" cy=\"147.48\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"520.90\" cy=\"752.52\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"173.48\" cy=\"515.90\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"826.52\" cy=\"384.10\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"434.10\" cy=\"147.48\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"565.90\" cy=\"752.52\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"127.48\" cy=\"470.90\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"872.52\" cy=\"429.10\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"479.10\" cy=\"107.48\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"520.90\" cy=\"792.52\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"127.48\" cy=\"515.90\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"872.52\" cy=\"384.10\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"434.10\" cy=\"107.48\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"565.90\" cy=\"792.52\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"81.48\" cy=\"470.90\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"918.52\" cy=\"429.10\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"479.10\" cy=\"67.48\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"520.90\" cy=\"832.52\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"81.48\" cy=\"515.90\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"918.52\" cy=\"384.10\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"434.10\" cy=\"67.48\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"565.90\" cy=\"832.52\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"35.48\" cy=\"470.90\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"964.52\" cy=\"429.10\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"479.10\" cy=\"27.48\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n          <circle class=\"trip-passenger\" cx=\"520.90\" cy=\"872.52\" r=\"2.4\"/>\n</g></g>\n      </svg>\n\n      <p class=\"trip-traffic-reading\" data-trip-traffic-reading hidden><strong>60 bus passengers.</strong><br>32 people in all those cars.</p><figcaption>Roof cutaways reveal the people inside. Every pale dot is a traveler. Cars include drivers; the bus adds an operator. An illustrative snapshot of queues at a four-way junction, not a measured road or traffic-flow simulation.</figcaption>\n      <p class=\"trip-sr\" role=\"status\" data-trip-status></p>\n    </figure>\n  </div></div>\n  <div class=\"trip-chapter\" id=\"trip-space\">\n    <div class=\"trip-chapter-head\"><h3>The people stay.<br>The cars don’t have to.</h3><div><p>Put those same 60 people four to a car: <strong>15 cars instead of 60.</strong> Give them a bus carrying 60 passengers: one passenger vehicle, with a driver too. The travelers have not vanished. The vehicles have.</p><p>A city’s job is to connect people to places. Counting how many cars get through tells only part of that story.</p></div></div>\n    <div class=\"trip-capacity\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Street capacity ranges in people per hour\">\n      <div class=\"trip-axis\"><span>People per hour · 0</span><span>25,000</span></div><div class=\"trip-lane\"><div class=\"trip-lane-label\"><span>Private motor vehicles</span><strong>600–1,600</strong></div><div class=\"trip-track\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span style=\"--low:2.4%;--high:6.4%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"trip-lane\"><div class=\"trip-lane-label\"><span>Two-way protected bikeway</span><strong>6,500–7,500</strong></div><div class=\"trip-track\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span style=\"--low:26.0%;--high:30.0%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"trip-lane\"><div class=\"trip-lane-label\"><span>Dedicated transit lanes</span><strong>4,000–8,000</strong></div><div class=\"trip-track\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span style=\"--low:16.0%;--high:32.0%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"trip-lane\"><div class=\"trip-lane-label\"><span>Sidewalk</span><strong>8,000–9,000</strong></div><div class=\"trip-track\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span style=\"--low:32.0%;--high:36.0%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"trip-lane\"><div class=\"trip-lane-label\"><span>On-street transitway, bus or rail</span><strong>10,000–25,000</strong></div><div class=\"trip-track\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span style=\"--low:40.0%;--high:100.0%\"></span></div></div>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"trip-note\">Each dark segment is a range, on the same zero-based scale. A 3-metre lane or equivalent width, at peak conditions with normal operations. These are design-guide capacities, not average ridership or a promise for every street. The private-vehicle range is not restricted to solo drivers. A two-way bikeway counts both directions. <a href=\"https://nacto.org/wp-content/uploads/Fabrizio_Prati_Safe_Speeds_NACTO18.pdf\">NACTO/GDCI, PDF p. 23</a>; <a href=\"https://globaldesigningcities.org/publication/global-street-design-guide/defining-streets/multimodal-streets-serve-people/\">scope and assumptions</a>.</p>\n    <p class=\"trip-note\">A high-capacity transitway is a different facility from a bus sharing traffic. Stops, signals, service frequency, occupancy and safe access all matter. Space for walking and cycling also means space for trips that need no car at all.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"trip-energy\" id=\"trip-energy\"><div class=\"trip-chapter\">\n    <div class=\"trip-chapter-head\"><h3>So much energy.<br>So little motion.</h3><p>For a conventional gasoline vehicle on the DOE/EPA combined city–highway cycle, about <strong>18–25% of the fuel’s energy reaches the wheels.</strong> Most is lost in the engine, primarily as heat. And the wheels move the whole vehicle, along with whoever is inside.</p></div>\n    <figure class=\"trip-energy-figure\" aria-labelledby=\"trip-energy-caption\">\n      <div class=\"trip-energy-flow\">\n        <div class=\"trip-energy-input\"><strong>100<span>%</span></strong><span>Energy in the fuel</span></div>\n        <svg class=\"trip-energy-ribbons\" viewBox=\"0 0 700 360\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n          <path class=\"trip-energy-lost-band\" d=\"M90 120H210C360 120 360 210 530 210H700V330H530C320 330 350 240 210 240H90Z\"/>\n          <path class=\"trip-energy-wheel-band\" d=\"M90 80H210C380 80 350 30 530 30H700V70H530C350 70 380 120 210 120H90Z\"/>\n          <path class=\"trip-energy-supply\" d=\"M0 80H90V240H0Z\"/>\n          <g class=\"trip-energy-streams\" fill=\"none\">\n            <path d=\"M90 100H210C380 100 350 50 530 50H696\"/>\n            <path d=\"M90 140H210C358 140 353 230 530 230H696\"/>\n            <path d=\"M90 180H210C355 180 340 270 530 270H696\"/>\n            <path d=\"M90 220H210C352 220 327 310 530 310H696\"/>\n          </g>\n        </svg>\n        <div class=\"trip-energy-output trip-energy-wheel\"><strong>25<span>%</span></strong><span>Reaches the wheels</span><small>The upper end of the estimate</small></div>\n        <div class=\"trip-energy-output trip-energy-lost\"><strong>75<span>%</span></strong><span>Losses &amp; other uses</span><small>Mostly lost in the engine as heat</small></div>\n      </div>\n      <figcaption id=\"trip-energy-caption\">Even at the upper end of the <strong>18–25% range</strong>, only one quarter of the fuel’s energy reaches the wheels.</figcaption>\n    </figure>\n    <p class=\"trip-note\">Band widths show the 25%-to-wheels case; the remaining 75% is 100% minus 25%. Estimated combined cycle: 55% city, 45% highway. A range across vehicles, not a measurement of your trip. <a href=\"https://www.fueleconomy.gov/feg/atv.shtml\">DOE/EPA: where the energy goes</a>.</p>\n    <div class=\"trip-energy-bottom\"><p><strong>Electric cars change this part of the story.</strong> DOE reports 87–91% efficiency including energy recovered through regenerative braking. That vehicle-level figure does not include electricity generation or manufacturing. Electrifying a car still leaves a car to accommodate.</p><p><strong>Occupancy changes the denominator.</strong> Energy per passenger-distance is vehicle energy divided by the people carried. A well-used bus can spread its energy use over many riders; a nearly empty one may not. Vehicle technology, route and occupancy decide the comparison. <a href=\"https://www.energy.gov/cmei/vehicles/articles/fotw-1360-sept-16-2024-typical-ev-87-91-efficient-compared-30-conventional\">DOE’s EV explanation</a>.</p></div>\n  </div></div>\n  <div class=\"trip-chapter\" id=\"trip-resources\">\n    <div class=\"trip-chapter-head\"><h3>Two tonnes.<br>One invitation to share.</h3><p>Imagine a car that weighs 2,000 kg before anyone gets in. Driving alone assigns all two tonnes of that machine to one traveler. Add people and the vehicle mass per person falls. Move the slider; the car stays the same.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"trip-mass\"><div>\n      <div class=\"trip-mass-reading\"><strong><span data-trip-mass-value>2,000</span> kg</strong><span>of vehicle per person</span></div>\n      <div data-trip-mass-control hidden><label>People sharing the car: <strong data-trip-mass-load>1 person in the car</strong><input data-trip-mass type=\"range\" min=\"1\" max=\"4\" step=\"1\" value=\"1\" aria-label=\"People sharing the two-tonne car\" aria-valuetext=\"1 person: 2000 kilograms of vehicle per person\"></label><div class=\"trip-mass-ends\"><span>1 person</span><span>4 people</span></div></div>\n      <p class=\"trip-note\">Illustrative 2,000 kg vehicle ÷ occupants, including driver. Not a fleet average, raw-material footprint or measure of energy efficiency. With four aboard: 500 kg per person.</p>\n    </div><div class=\"trip-prose\"><p>That machine has to be manufactured before it ever moves. Sharing an existing car uses the same machine for more people on that trip. It does not retroactively erase the materials used to build it.</p><p>Replacing solo trips with fewer vehicles creates an opportunity to need fewer vehicles—and less space to store them. The actual material savings depend on what is built, how long it lasts, and whether shared travel really replaces car ownership and driving.</p><p>Walking and cycling change the scale of the machine needed. Making them useful options takes safe, connected routes and destinations people can reach.</p></div></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"trip-city-section\" id=\"trip-neighborhood\">\n    <div class=\"trip-city-heading\"><h3>Bring life<br>closer.</h3><p>More homes near the places we need.<br>Public transit that makes the connection.<br>A neighborhood built around living.</p></div>\n    <figure class=\"trip-neighborhood\" aria-labelledby=\"trip-city-caption\">\n      <div class=\"trip-city-controls\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Explore the neighborhood model\" hidden><button type=\"button\" data-city-view=\"neighborhood\" aria-pressed=\"true\">The whole neighborhood</button><button type=\"button\" data-city-view=\"transit\" aria-pressed=\"false\">The transit street</button><button type=\"button\" data-city-view=\"homes\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Homes over shops</button></div>\n      <div class=\"trip-city-field\"><canvas hidden aria-hidden=\"true\"></canvas><div class=\"trip-city-fallback\"><p>Homes above shops. A bus corridor between them. Connected sidewalks, protected cycling and safe crossings make the neighborhood a whole.</p><p>The interactive model is optional; the design principles are explained below.</p></div><span class=\"trip-city-model-label\">An imagined neighborhood</span></div>\n      <figcaption id=\"trip-city-caption\" data-city-caption aria-live=\"polite\">Homes above shops. A transit corridor through the middle. Walking and cycling routes that join it all up.</figcaption>\n      <p class=\"trip-city-qualification\">Original design sketch, not a real place or a simulation. Building heights, people, vehicles and distances are illustrative. No travel-time, housing-capacity or emissions result is implied. Trader Joe’s sign identifies an illustrative shop; this is not an actual store or an endorsement.</p>\n    </figure>\n    <div class=\"trip-city-argument\"><div><h4>Put more life<br>within reach.</h4><p>Allow more homes near shops, services and transit. Mix uses. Connect the blocks. Compact, mixed-use neighborhoods make walking, cycling and transit more convenient. Density can look like the apartments above a grocer, the rowhomes around a corner, the everyday places along a bus route.</p></div><div><h4>Make transit<br>worth choosing.</h4><p>A painted lane is only a start. The proposal here pairs dedicated bus space with frequent, reliable service, accessible stops and safe crossings. Build the neighborhood and fund the service together. <a href=\"https://www.epa.gov/smartgrowth/smart-growth-and-transportation\">EPA: connecting land use and transportation</a>.</p></div></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"trip-chapter trip-closing\">\n    <div class=\"trip-prose\"><h3>The goal is a place<br>you can live in.</h3><p>Make room for more neighbors, different housing needs, and the people already there. Keep access for disabled people, deliveries and trips that still need a car. Give people a real alternative for the trips that do not.</p><p class=\"trip-pull\">A home near daily life.<br>A bus worth waiting for.<br>A street worth walking down.</p><p>Inspired by Bill Nye’s <a href=\"https://www.billnye.com/the-science-guy/human-transportation\"><cite>Human Transportation</cite></a>. The next step is a question of design: how do we make it easier to get where life happens?</p></div>\n    <details class=\"trip-receipts\" id=\"trip-sources\"><summary>The sources, the assumptions, the numbers</summary><div class=\"trip-methods\">\n      <p><strong>Different questions, different measures.</strong> The opening counts vehicles. The lane chart compares people per hour. The energy graphic compares fuel energy reaching the wheels. The mass slider divides an assumed vehicle mass by occupants. None is a complete environmental score.</p>\n      <table><caption>Illustrative group of 60 travelers</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Occupancy</th><th scope=\"col\">Vehicles</th><th scope=\"col\">Vehicle kg/person</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">1 per car</th><td>60</td><td>2,000</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2 per car</th><td>30</td><td>1,000</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">3 per car</th><td>20</td><td>666.67</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">4 per car</th><td>15</td><td>500</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">60 bus passengers</th><td>1 bus</td><td>Not modeled</td></tr></tbody></table>\n      <p>Car calculations: 60 ÷ occupants; 2,000 kg ÷ occupants. Slider rounds to the nearest kg. The bus adds an operator; it does not claim every bus has this capacity or occupancy. Vehicle silhouettes are schematic, with no distance, area or traffic-gap scale.</p>\n      <table><caption>Design-guide capacity · people/hour per 3-m lane or equivalent width</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Mode/facility</th><th scope=\"col\">Range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Private motor vehicles</th><td>600–1,600</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Two-way protected bikeway</th><td>6,500–7,500</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Dedicated transit lanes</th><td>4,000–8,000</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Sidewalk</th><td>8,000–9,000</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">On-street transitway, bus or rail</th><td>10,000–25,000</td></tr></tbody></table>\n      <p>Ranges are planning guidance from the 2016 <cite>Global Street Design Guide</cite>, reproduced in Fabrizio Prati’s 2018 NACTO/GDCI presentation, PDF page 23. They are not measured 2026 flows or confidence intervals. Facility types and directions differ as labeled; the two-way bikeway includes both directions. No minimum/maximum ratio is presented as a guaranteed improvement.</p>\n      <p>Gasoline: DOE/EPA’s combined-cycle diagram estimates 18–25% to the wheels; the page’s broader 12–30% headline spans different drive cycles. EV: DOE’s September 16, 2024 summary reports 65–69% to wheels plus about 22% net regenerative recovery, yielding 87–91%. Do not multiply a passenger’s fraction of vehicle mass by either efficiency figure: drag, rolling resistance and other loads make that an invalid allocation of useful energy.</p>\n      <a href=\"/assets/transport-data.csv\" download>Download values and assumptions (CSV)</a>\n      <ul><li><a href=\"https://globaldesigningcities.org/publication/global-street-design-guide/defining-streets/multimodal-streets-serve-people/\">Global Street Design Guide — operating conditions and lane width</a></li><li><a href=\"https://nacto.org/wp-content/uploads/Fabrizio_Prati_Safe_Speeds_NACTO18.pdf\">NACTO/GDCI presentation — capacity graphic, PDF page 23</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.fueleconomy.gov/feg/atv.shtml\">DOE/EPA — gasoline energy flows, combined city/highway diagram</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.energy.gov/cmei/vehicles/articles/fotw-1360-sept-16-2024-typical-ev-87-91-efficient-compared-30-conventional\">DOE — EV efficiency including regenerative braking, 2024</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.epa.gov/smartgrowth/smart-growth-and-transportation\">EPA — compact development, mixed uses and transit-oriented neighborhoods</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.billnye.com/the-science-guy/human-transportation\">Bill Nye — official episode page, inspiration rather than a numerical source</a></li></ul>\n      <p>The neighborhood is an original architectural design sketch. Its view buttons change the camera, not measured conditions or predicted outcomes. The qualitative relationship between compact mixed-use development and transportation options follows EPA guidance; the proposal for investment and service is editorial. The Trader Joe’s logo is sourced from its official store-location site and belongs to Trader Joe’s; no affiliation is implied. All other illustrations and calculations are original to weird.stats. No episode footage or source artwork is reproduced. Sources checked October 6, 2026. This essay combines illustrative models, engineering estimates and editorial interpretation; it does not estimate a city’s emissions savings.</p>\n    </div></details>\n    <a class=\"trip-next\" href=\"{{nextHref}}\">Back to the wandering</a>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
